\begin{abstract}
Let $S$ be a finite nonempty set of voters, let $\delta : S \to \R$ be a system of weights, and let a \emph{profile} be a vector $x$ of scores. The min-plus (tropical) aggregator $F(x) = \min_{i \in S}(x_i + \delta_i)$ partitions profile space into cells according to the \emph{decisive set} $\Dec(x) = \{i \in S : x_i + \delta_i = F(x)\}$; the resulting chamber complex is the normal fan of a simplex, and is the social-choice avatar of a tropical hyperplane. We prove a complete theory of exchange in this complex. First, a sharp trichotomy: from a profile in the open chamber of an incumbent $i$, resetting the single score of a voter $j \neq i$ to $c$ lands in the open cell $\{j\}$ iff $c < \theta$, on the wall $\{i,j\}$ iff $c = \theta$, and in the incumbent's chamber iff $c \ge \theta$, where $\theta = x_i + \delta_i - \delta_j$; along the exchange segment the aggregate is $x_i + \delta_i - \max(t,0)$, piecewise affine with a single kink at the wall; and a converse holds, so that a downward single-voter move between chambers must be a move of the incoming winner. Second, a metric theory: the minimum number of voters who must lower their scores to move from the open chamber of $i$ to the cell labelled $T$ is exactly $|T \setminus \{i\}|$, an attained optimum, which for faces of the incumbent's chamber equals the codimension of the target cell; the downwardness hypothesis is shown to be essential by a two-voter counterexample. The dual graph of chambers is complete, the $f$-vector is $f_d = \binom{|S|}{d+1}$, the complex has $2^{|S|}-1$ cells, and the alternating sum of the $f$-vector is $1$. Third, a rigidity dichotomy: the aggregator as a function determines $(S,\delta)$ exactly, whereas the cell labelling determines $S$ exactly and $\delta$ exactly up to a single global additive constant, the gauge group being precisely $\R \cdot \mathbf{1}$. We close with a Pythagorean instantiation in which the relation $a^2+b^2=c^2$ forces a strictly positive exchange gap, interpreted as a stability margin.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} min-plus algebra, tropical hyperplane, chamber complex, exchange law, normal fan, $f$-vector, rigidity, social choice.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The object of study}

Min-plus (equivalently, tropical) linear forms are ubiquitous: they compute shortest paths, bottleneck capacities, makespans, and the linear regions of rectified piecewise-linear functions. Fix a finite index set $\iota$ of \emph{voters}, a nonempty \emph{support} $S \subseteq \iota$, and \emph{weights} $\delta : \iota \to \R$. A \emph{profile} is a function $x : \iota \to \R$. The \textbf{aggregator} is
\[
F(x) \;=\; \min_{i \in S} \bigl( x_i + \delta_i \bigr).
\]
Reading $x_i$ as the score reported by voter $i$ and $\delta_i$ as a fixed handicap, $F$ is the weakest-link or egalitarian bottleneck welfare function. Writing $\oplus$ for $\min$ and $\odot$ for $+$, $F(x) = \bigoplus_{i \in S} \delta_i \odot x_i$ is a tropical linear form in which every monomial is essential.

\begin{definition}[Decisive set]
For a profile $x$, the \emph{decisive set} is $\Dec(x) = \{ i \in S : x_i + \delta_i = F(x) \}$, a nonempty subset of $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chamber]
For $i \in S$, the \emph{chamber} of $i$ is
$C_i = \{ x : x_i + \delta_i \le x_k + \delta_k \text{ for all } k \in S \}$.
Thus $i \in \Dec(x)$ iff $i \in S$ and $x \in C_i$, and $F(x) = x_i + \delta_i$ on $C_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cells and chamber complex]
For nonempty $T \subseteq S$, the \emph{open cell} labelled $T$ is $\{x : \Dec(x) = T\}$ and the \emph{closed cell} is $\bigcap_{i \in T} C_i = \{ x : T \subseteq \Dec(x)\}$. The collection of closed cells, indexed by the nonempty subsets of $S$, is the \emph{chamber complex} of $(S,\delta)$.
\end{definition}

The closed cell labelled $T$ is cut out by the $|T|-1$ independent equations $x_i + \delta_i = x_j + \delta_j$ ($i,j \in T$) together with inequalities; writing $L_T$ for its direction (lineality) space and $n = |\iota|$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dim}
\dim L_T \;=\; n - |T| + 1,
\end{equation}
so the cell labelled $T$ has codimension $|T| - 1$. Chambers are top-dimensional; walls, of codimension one, are labelled by pairs.

\subsection{The exchange question}

The natural social-choice avatar of a gallery step is an \emph{exchange}: one voter changes their score and the winner changes. We make the avatar exact: sharpness (\S\ref{sec:sharp}), a metric (\S\ref{sec:metric}), the combinatorics of the complex (\S\ref{sec:comb}), and rigidity (\S\ref{sec:rigid}). Every statement is an equivalence or an attained optimum wherever one is available; Theorem~\ref{thm:counter} flags the single place where a hypothesis cannot be weakened.

Throughout, $\upd(x;j,c)$ is the profile equal to $x$ except in coordinate $j$, where it takes the value $c$.

\section{The master formula}

\begin{theorem}[Master formula]\label{thm:master}
Let $j \in S$ with $S \setminus \{j\} \neq \emptyset$. Then for all $c \in \R$,
\[
F\bigl(\upd(x;j,c)\bigr) \;=\; \min\Bigl( c + \delta_j,\ \min_{k \in S \setminus \{j\}} (x_k + \delta_k)\Bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides are minima over $S$ of the same family of affine functions, split according to whether the index equals $j$. For ``$\le$'' evaluate the left side at $j$ and at each $k \neq j$; for ``$\ge$'' bound below term-by-term, using that the update leaves coordinates $k \neq j$ untouched.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Master formula on a chamber]\label{thm:masterchamber}
Let $i,j \in S$ with $i \neq j$ and $x \in C_i$. Then for all $c \in \R$,
\[
F\bigl(\upd(x;j,c)\bigr) \;=\; \min\bigl( c + \delta_j,\ x_i + \delta_i \bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
``$\le$'' follows by testing at $j$ and at $i$, the latter using $\upd(x;j,c)_i = x_i$. ``$\ge$'': for $k \in S$, either $k = j$, giving the first term, or $k \neq j$ and then $x_k + \delta_k \ge x_i + \delta_i$ because $x \in C_i$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:masterchamber} exhibits the aggregate along a single-coordinate exchange as the minimum of two affine functions of $c$, one of slope $1$ and one constant. The rest of \S\ref{sec:sharp} is bookkeeping around their crossing point.

\section{The sharp single-voter exchange law}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{definition}[Exchange threshold]
$\theta_{ij}(x) = x_i + \delta_i - \delta_j$, the score that $j$ must be given to tie exactly with $i$ at $x$.
\end{definition}

Throughout this section $i,j \in S$, $i \neq j$, $x \in C_i$, $\theta = \theta_{ij}(x)$ and $y_c = \upd(x;j,c)$.

\begin{theorem}[Challenger's half]\label{thm:chal}
$j \in \Dec(y_c) \iff c \le \theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:masterchamber}, $j \in \Dec(y_c)$ means $c + \delta_j = \min(c+\delta_j, x_i + \delta_i)$, i.e.\ $c + \delta_j \le x_i + \delta_i$, i.e.\ $c \le \theta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Incumbent's half]\label{thm:inc}
$i \in \Dec(y_c) \iff \theta \le c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Symmetrically, $i \in \Dec(y_c)$ means $x_i + \delta_i \le c + \delta_j$, i.e.\ $\theta \le c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp exchange law]\label{thm:sharp}
$\Dec(y_c) = \{j\} \iff c < \theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $c \ge \theta$ then $i \in \Dec(y_c)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:inc}, contradicting $\Dec(y_c)=\{j\}$ as $i \neq j$. Conversely if $c < \theta$ then for every $k \neq j$ in $S$ we have $c + \delta_j < x_i + \delta_i \le x_k + \delta_k$, so $j$ is the strict minimizer.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp wall law]\label{thm:wall}
$\{i,j\} \subseteq \Dec(y_c) \iff c = \theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Theorems~\ref{thm:chal} and~\ref{thm:inc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
revision $c$ & decisive set \\
\midrule
$c > \theta$ & contains $i$, not $j$: incumbent's chamber \\
$c = \theta$ & contains $i$ and $j$: the wall $\{i,j\}$ \\
$c < \theta$ & equals $\{j\}$: challenger's open chamber \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{The kink of the aggregate}

\begin{definition}[Exchange path]
For $t \in \R$ let $\gamma(t) = \upd(x;j,\theta - t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Piecewise-affine aggregate]\label{thm:path}
For $x \in C_i$, $i \neq j$ in $S$, and all $t \in \R$,
\[
F(\gamma(t)) \;=\; x_i + \delta_i - \max(t,0).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:masterchamber}, $F(\gamma(t)) = \min(x_i + \delta_i - t,\ x_i+\delta_i)$, and $\min(u-t,u) = u - \max(t,0)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The kink is genuine]\label{cor:kink}
For every $t > 0$, $F(\gamma(-t)) = F(\gamma(0))$ and $F(\gamma(0)) - F(\gamma(t)) = t$. Hence the left slope at $0$ is $0$, the right slope is $-1$, and the aggregate is not differentiable at the wall.
\end{corollary}

The wall is therefore precisely the non-differentiability locus of the aggregate along the exchange direction --- the min-plus analogue of the activation boundary of a rectified linear unit. On the incumbent's side the parameter $t$ is pure slack; on the challenger's side it is transmitted to the social score one-for-one.

\subsection{Comparative statics and the converse}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $x_k \le y_k$ for all $k$ then $F(x) \le F(y)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[No spurious pivots]\label{thm:nospur}
If $c \le x_k$, then $\Dec(\upd(x;k,c)) \subseteq \{k\} \cup \Dec(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $m \neq k$ be decisive after the move. The move is downward so $F$ weakly decreases, while $m$'s monomial is untouched; hence $x_m + \delta_m = F(\upd(x;k,c)) \le F(x) \le x_m + \delta_m$, forcing $m \in \Dec(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Raising cannot unseat a bystander]\label{thm:raise}
If $x_k \le c$, then $\Dec(x)\setminus\{k\} \subseteq \Dec(\upd(x;k,c))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Converse of the exchange law]\label{thm:conv}
Suppose $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$, $c \le x_k$, and $\Dec(\upd(x;k,c)) = \{j\}$ with $j \neq i$. Then $k = j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:nospur}, $j \in \{k\}\cup\{i\}$; as $j \neq i$, $j = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange law, both directions]\label{thm:iff}
Let $i,j \in S$, $i \neq j$, $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and $c \le x_k$. Then
\[
\Dec\bigl(\upd(x;k,c)\bigr) = \{j\} \iff \bigl(k = j \ \text{ and }\ c < \theta_{ij}(x)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Coalition exchanges}

\begin{definition}[Coalition exchange]
For $x \in C_i$, $T \subseteq S$ and $\varepsilon \in \R$, set
\[
E_T^{\varepsilon}(x)_k = \begin{cases} x_i + \delta_i - \delta_k - \varepsilon, & k \in T,\\ x_k, & k \notin T.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Coalition exchange law]\label{thm:coal}
For $x \in C_i$, nonempty $T \subseteq S$ and $\varepsilon > 0$, $\Dec(E_T^{\varepsilon}(x)) = T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $k \in T$ has monomial exactly $x_i + \delta_i - \varepsilon$; each $k \in S \setminus T$ has monomial $x_k + \delta_k \ge x_i + \delta_i > x_i+\delta_i-\varepsilon$. Hence the aggregate is $x_i+\delta_i-\varepsilon$ and the argmin is $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Wall case]\label{thm:coalwall}
For $i \in S$, $x \in C_i$ and $T \subseteq S$, $\Dec(E_T^{0}(x)) = T \cup \Dec(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Reachability]
From any $x \in C_i$ and any nonempty $T \subseteq S$ there is a profile $y$ with $\Dec(y) = T$ and $y_k = x_k$ for all $k \notin T$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Lipschitz control}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:lip}
If $|x_k - y_k| \le M$ for all $k$ then $|F(x)-F(y)| \le M$. In particular
$|F(\upd(x;j,c)) - F(x)| \le |c - x_j|$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The exchange metric}\label{sec:metric}

Fix $x$ with $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and consider \emph{downward moves}: profiles $y$ with $y_k \le x_k$ for all $k$. The \emph{exchange support} is a set $D$ with $y_k = x_k$ for all $k \notin D$.

\begin{theorem}[Locality of downward moves]\label{thm:local}
If $y_k \le x_k$ for all $k$ and $y_k = x_k$ for all $k \notin D$, then $\Dec(y) \subseteq D \cup \Dec(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $m \in \Dec(y)$ with $m \notin D$. Then $y \le x$ pointwise gives $F(y) \le F(x)$, and $y_m = x_m$, so $x_m + \delta_m = F(y) \le F(x) \le x_m + \delta_m$; equality throughout gives $m \in \Dec(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
If $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$, $y$ is a downward move with support $D$ and $\Dec(y) = T$, then $T \setminus \{i\} \subseteq D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{thm:upper}
If $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and $T \subseteq S$ is nonempty, there is a downward move $y \le x$ with $y_k = x_k$ for all $k \notin T \setminus \{i\}$ and $\Dec(y) = T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $i \in T$, take $y = E^{0}_{T \setminus \{i\}}(x)$: each mover receives $x_i + \delta_i - \delta_k \le x_k$ (since $x \in C_i$), so the move is downward, and Theorem~\ref{thm:coalwall} gives $\Dec(y) = (T \setminus \{i\}) \cup \{i\} = T$. If $i \notin T$, take $y = E^{1}_{T}(x)$: each mover receives $x_i + \delta_i - \delta_k - 1 < x_k$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:coal} gives $\Dec(y) = T$, with $T \setminus \{i\} = T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange distance]\label{thm:dist}
Let $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and let $T \subseteq S$ be nonempty. Then $|T \setminus \{i\}|$ is the least element of
\[
\bigl\{\, m \in \N : \exists D,\ \exists y,\ |D| = m,\ y \le x,\ y|_{D^c} = x|_{D^c},\ \Dec(y) = T \,\bigr\}.
\]
That is, the exchange distance from the open chamber of $i$ to the cell labelled $T$ is exactly $|T \setminus \{i\}|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Membership is Theorem~\ref{thm:upper} with $D = T \setminus \{i\}$; minimality is Theorem~\ref{thm:lower} together with monotonicity of cardinality under inclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange distance equals codimension]\label{thm:codim}
If in addition $i \in T$, so that the cell labelled $T$ is a face of the chamber of $i$, the exchange distance to it equals $n - \dim L_T = |T| - 1$, the codimension of the cell in profile space.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|T\setminus\{i\}| = |T|-1$ since $i \in T$, and $\dim L_T = n - |T| + 1$ by \eqref{eq:dim}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Downwardness is essential}

\begin{theorem}[Counterexample for upward moves]\label{thm:counter}
There exist two voters, weights $\delta$, profiles $x,y$ and a set $D$ with
$\Dec(x) = \{0\}$, $\Dec(y) = \{1\}$, $y_k = x_k$ for all $k \notin D$ and $x_k \le y_k$ for all $k$, yet $\{1\}\setminus\{0\} \not\subseteq D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $S = \{0,1\}$, $\delta = (0,1)$, $x = (0,0)$, $y = (2,0)$, $D = \{0\}$. Then $F(x) = \min(0,1) = 0$ is attained only at $0$ and $F(y) = \min(2,1) = 1$ only at $1$; the move is upward and supported on $\{0\}$, yet the winner is voter $1$, who never moved.
\end{proof}

The asymmetry is structural: a downward move can only lower the aggregate, promoting the mover alone, whereas an upward move can lift the aggregate to meet a stationary monomial, promoting a bystander.

\section{The dual graph, the $f$-vector, and the Euler characteristic}\label{sec:comb}

\begin{definition}[Exchange graph]
Vertices $S$; $i \sim j$ iff $i \neq j$ and some profile has $\Dec(x) = \{i,j\}$, i.e.\ the chambers of $i$ and $j$ share a wall.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness]\label{thm:complete}
The exchange graph is the complete graph on $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Given $i \neq j$, take $x_i = 0$, $x_j = \delta_i - \delta_j$ and $x_k$ large for $k \notin \{i,j\}$; then $\Dec(x) = \{i,j\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The exchange graph is connected and is a single clique on $S$: the chamber complex is gallery-connected of diameter one. Any incumbent may be replaced by any challenger through a single wall crossing.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[$f$-vector]\label{thm:fvec}
The number of cells of codimension $d$ is $f_d = \binom{|S|}{d+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total count]\label{thm:count}
The complex has exactly $2^{|S|}-1$ cells, one for each nonempty subset of $S$, and distinct labels give distinct cells.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler contractibility]\label{thm:euler}
\[
\sum_{\emptyset \neq T \subseteq S} (-1)^{|T|+1} \;=\; \sum_{d \ge 0} (-1)^d \binom{|S|}{d+1} \;=\; 1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sum_{T \subseteq S} (-1)^{|T|} = (1-1)^{|S|} = 0$ for nonempty $S$; splitting off $T = \emptyset$ gives $\sum_{T \neq \emptyset}(-1)^{|T|} = -1$, and negating yields the claim.
\end{proof}

The value $1$ is the Euler characteristic of a point, as it must be for a complete fan. The three results are shadows of one fact: the chamber complex of a min-plus linear form is the normal fan of a simplex on $|S|$ vertices, whose faces are the nonempty subsets of the vertex set.

\section{Rigidity: what the geometry remembers}\label{sec:rigid}

\begin{lemma}[Spike evaluation]\label{lem:spike}
Let $i \in S$ and let $c$ satisfy $c + \delta_i \le \delta_k$ for all $k \in S$. Let $x$ be $0$ everywhere except $x_i = c$. Then $F(x) = c + \delta_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Invisible voters]\label{lem:invis}
If $i \notin S$, the same spike profile satisfies $F(x) = F(0) = \min_{k \in S}\delta_k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The support is determined]\label{thm:suppdet}
If $F_{S,\delta} = F_{S',\delta'}$ as functions on profiles, then $S = S'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $i \in S \setminus S'$, drive $x_i$ to $-\infty$ along spikes: by Lemma~\ref{lem:spike} the left aggregate tends to $-\infty$, while by Lemma~\ref{lem:invis} the right aggregate is constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The weights are determined]\label{thm:wdet}
If $F_{S,\delta} = F_{S',\delta'}$ and $i \in S$, then $\delta_i = \delta'_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Functional rigidity]\label{thm:frigid}
Two min-plus aggregators define the same function on profiles if and only if they have the same support and the same weights on it: no monomial is redundant, because every voter of the support is decisive somewhere.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Gauge invariance]\label{thm:gauge}
For every constant $c$ and every profile $x$, $\Dec_{S,\delta+c\mathbf 1}(x) = \Dec_{S,\delta}(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$F_{\delta+c\mathbf 1}(x) = F_\delta(x) + c$, and every monomial is shifted by the same $c$, so the argmin is unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The labelling determines the support]\label{thm:labsupp}
If $\Dec_{S,\delta}(x) = \Dec_{S',\delta'}(x)$ for every profile $x$, then $S = S'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The labelling determines the weights up to a constant]\label{thm:labw}
If $\Dec_{S,\delta}(x) = \Dec_{S,\delta'}(x)$ for every $x$, then $\delta_i - \delta'_i = \delta_j - \delta'_j$ for all $i,j \in S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose $x$ with $\Dec_{S,\delta}(x) = \{i,j\}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:complete}); then also $\Dec_{S,\delta'}(x) = \{i,j\}$, so $x_i + \delta_i = x_j + \delta_j$ and $x_i + \delta'_i = x_j + \delta'_j$; subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Combinatorial rigidity, both directions]\label{thm:crigid}
Two weight systems on the same support induce the same chamber complex if and only if they differ by a single global constant on the support:
\[
\bigl(\forall x,\ \Dec_{S,\delta}(x) = \Dec_{S,\delta'}(x)\bigr) \iff \exists c \in \R\ \forall k \in S,\ \delta_k = \delta'_k + c .
\]
\end{theorem}

Thus the map $(S,\delta) \mapsto F$ is injective, while the map $(S,\delta)\mapsto(\text{chamber complex})$ has fibres exactly the lines $\delta + \R\cdot\mathbf 1$. The complex is a complete invariant of the electorate modulo the one gauge freedom that no observer of \emph{who wins} could detect, and the numerical aggregator rigidifies that gauge.

\section{A Pythagorean instantiation and stability margins}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:pyth}
If $a,b,c > 0$ and $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, then $a < c$ and $b < c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:neutral}
If $\delta_i \le \delta_j$ for all $j \in S$, then the neutral profile $0$ lies in $C_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Pythagorean exchange gap]\label{thm:pyth}
Weight three voters by the sides of a Pythagorean triple: $\delta = (a,b,c)$ with $a,b,c>0$, $a \le b$ and $a^2+b^2=c^2$. Then at the neutral profile $0$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item voter $0$ (the leg $a$) is decisive, $0 \in C_0$;
\item the exchange threshold for the hypotenuse voter is $\theta_{02}(0) = a - c$;
\item the exchange gap is strictly positive, $c - a > 0$;
\item for every $\varepsilon > 0$, giving the hypotenuse voter the score $a - c - \varepsilon$ makes its decisive set $\{2\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) By Lemma~\ref{lem:pyth} $a$ is the least weight; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:neutral}. (2) By definition. (3) Lemma~\ref{lem:pyth}. (4) Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, since $a-c-\varepsilon < a-c = \theta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $(3,4,5)$ instance]
With weights $(3,4,5)$, the neutral profile is decided by the leg-$3$ voter; the hypotenuse voter must revise its score to exactly $-2$ to tie and to anything strictly below $-2$ to win outright.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Gaps as stability margins]
Let $x$ have a unique decisive voter $i$ with margin $\mu = \min_{k \neq i}(x_k+\delta_k) - (x_i+\delta_i) > 0$. If $\|x - x'\|_\infty < \mu/2$, every monomial moves by less than $\mu/2$, so $\Dec(x') = \{i\}$: the open chamber contains the sup-ball of radius $\mu/2$ about $x$. Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:lip} this makes the system stable under bounded perturbation, with the exchange thresholds as the exact stability radii. At the neutral profile the margin is precisely the smallest weight gap.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n = |S|$.

\paragraph{A. Decisive set.} Compute $m = \min_{k\in S}(x_k+\delta_k)$ and return $\{k : x_k+\delta_k = m\}$. Cost $\Theta(n)$.

\paragraph{B. Exchange threshold and trichotomy.} Given $x$ with $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and a challenger $j$: return $\theta = x_i+\delta_i-\delta_j$ and classify $c$ as incumbent retained ($c>\theta$), wall ($c=\theta$), or challenger wins ($c<\theta$). Cost $\Theta(1)$ after $O(n)$ preprocessing.

\paragraph{C. Minimum-cost exchange to a target cell.} Given $x$ with $\Dec(x) = \{i\}$ and nonempty $T \subseteq S$: the mover set is $M = T \setminus \{i\}$, of size the exchange distance, and
\[
y_k = \begin{cases} x_i + \delta_i - \delta_k, & k \in M,\ i \in T,\\ x_i + \delta_i - \delta_k - \varepsilon, & k \in M,\ i \notin T\ (\varepsilon>0),\\ x_k, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
By Theorems~\ref{thm:lower}--\ref{thm:dist} this is optimal and $\Dec(y) = T$. Cost $\Theta(n)$.

\paragraph{D. Weight recovery from the labelling.} Recover $S$ by testing which singletons occur as labels; normalize $\delta_{i_0}=0$; recover each $\delta_i$ by binary search for the value $\tau$ making the label of a two-voter probe equal $\{i,i_0\}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:crigid} this determines $\delta$ up to the normalized constant, and no better.

\paragraph{E. $f$-vector.} Return $f_d = \binom{n}{d+1}$, total $2^n-1$, alternating sum $1$.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Shortest paths and routing.} In $d_v = \min_{u\to v}(d_u + w_{uv})$ the incoming edges are the voters and the edge weights the handicaps; the decisive set is the set of optimal predecessors and walls are ties. The exchange threshold is the reduced cost of a non-optimal predecessor, and Theorem~\ref{thm:conv} says a reroute onto a specific predecessor requires changing that predecessor's data.

\textbf{Scheduling.} The exchange threshold of a non-critical task is its total slack; Corollary~\ref{cor:kink} says slack is invisible until exhausted, whereupon delay is transmitted one-for-one.

\textbf{Piecewise-linear networks.} Chambers are linear regions and walls activation boundaries; Theorems~\ref{thm:fvec}--\ref{thm:euler} count the faces, the trichotomy identifies which coordinate crosses which boundary, and Theorem~\ref{thm:lip} is the tight local sensitivity.

\textbf{Social choice and procurement.} For the egalitarian bottleneck rule, the threshold is the exact concession needed to unseat the incumbent and Theorem~\ref{thm:iff} is a strong locality statement. Theorem~\ref{thm:crigid} says an observer of winners alone learns the weights up to a uniform shift.

\section{Discussion}

Three features deserve emphasis. \emph{Everything is an equivalence}: the classical exchange statements are upgraded to biconditionals (Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp}, \ref{thm:wall}, \ref{thm:iff}) and the metric statement to an attained least element (Theorem~\ref{thm:dist}); sharpness is what allows $|T\setminus\{i\}|$ to be \emph{identified} with codimension rather than merely bounded by it. \emph{Downwardness is a real hypothesis}, by Theorem~\ref{thm:counter}. \emph{Rigidity is exactly one-dimensional}: the gauge group is $\R\cdot\mathbf 1$ and nothing larger, the same degeneracy that makes tropical projective space the natural home of these fans.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Realization]
A complete polyhedral fan in $\R^n/\R\cdot\mathbf 1$ arises as the chamber complex of some min-plus aggregator if and only if it is the normal fan of a simplex; equivalently, iff its dual graph is complete and its $f$-vector is $\bigl(\binom{n}{d+1}\bigr)_d$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Spectral gap and stability]
For weights $\delta$ with pairwise gaps at least $\gamma > 0$, the incumbent's chamber contains a sup-ball of radius $\gamma/2$ around every profile whose label is a singleton.
\end{conjecture}

Further targets: a two-sided exchange metric charging bystander promotions correctly, in the light of Theorem~\ref{thm:counter}; the extension of Theorems~\ref{thm:dist}, \ref{thm:complete} and~\ref{thm:crigid} from tropical linear forms to general tropical hypersurfaces, where not every subset of monomials labels a cell; and a stochastic theory in which the profile is random and the exchange thresholds determine the boundaries of the events $\{\Dec = T\}$, connecting the exchange law to first-passage times.

